WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.100
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.567
is an Emmy-winning
and Tony-nominated

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.934
comedian and writer you know
her from her work on "SNL,"

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:09.400
"30 Rock," and "Girls5eva,"
as well as films

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.367
such as "Mean Girls"
and "Sisters."

00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:14.501
You can see her
alongside Amy Poehler

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.634
on their upcoming
Restless Leg Tour.

00:00:16.634 --> 00:00:19.434
For more information,
go to TinaAmyTour.com.

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:21.734
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:23.734
Tina Fey, everybody!

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:25.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:40.400
Welcome back!

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.033
-Oh, thank you.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:45.067
-Now, one could say this
is a promotional appearance

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:47.868
for your new tour,
but it's already worthless.

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:51.667
-Yes. I got an e-mail last night
saying, "Don't promote the tour.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.000
The tour is sold out!"
-Amazing!

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.067
This is two days.
-The tour is sold out.

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.100
-Two days?
-In two days, yes.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.534
So, these first four cities
are sold out.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:02.100
So I have no reason
to talk to you.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.901
-Okay. Well...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:05.133
-[ Laughs ]
-And this is always --

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.634
You've made it very clear
from the day we met

00:01:06.634 --> 00:01:09.100
all our conversations
had to be quid pro quo, yeah.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.300
-Yes, yeah.
-But this is so exciting.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.567
Obviously, you and Amy have
known each other for 30 years.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.300
-Yeah.
-It seems --

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.834
It's hard not to think of
Steve and Marty,

00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:19.033
Steve Martin and Marty Short,

00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:20.934
who would go out
and do a show like this.

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:22.234
Did you talk to them about it?

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:24.267
-I mean, obviously,
Steve Martin and Martin Short

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.667
were kind of
the inspiration for this,

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.100
because they so these live shows
because they just love

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.567
hanging out with each other.

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:31.133
And so we thought,
"Maybe, you know, we could do

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.534
something like that."

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:36.734
And the older that we get,
the more Marty looks like me.

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:38.100
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.434
So I was like,
"Yeah, we could do this."

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:42.067
And I did, actually --
They're so lovely, those guys.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.367
And I did hear from them.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.567
Got a lovely e-mail from
Steve Martin, that said, like,

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:46.868
you know, about
the announcements that,

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:49.200
"We're so excited for you,
and if you want to help

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.434
promote the show, you could say,
'This tour will be just like

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:55.133
Steve and Marty's tour,
but without the old-man smell.'"

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:56.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.334 --> 00:01:57.801
Which I thought that was so --
-Very, very good.

00:01:57.801 --> 00:02:00.234
-But I do think we will have a
fair amount of old-man smell.

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:01.634
-And have you heard from Marty?

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.634
-Yeah, well, Marty of course
had to chime in on the chain

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.300
and said like, "So excited.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.601
I can't wait to see which one
of you will turn out to be

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.033
the Steve of the duo

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:11.133
and which one will
entertain the audience."

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:12.667
-Perfect.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:15.000
Perfect Marty joke.
-Perfect...

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.901
-I will say Restless Leg Tour,
an excellent title.

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:19.267
-Poehler.
-Poehler came up with it?

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:20.400
Did you bandy about
a bunch of names?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.868
-We had a long list of names.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.067
My friend, Janine Brito,
said we should call it like

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.467
"'90s, 2000s, and Today,"

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:29.334
like a contemporary
radio station.

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:31.534
But we had, yeah,
all kinds of different things,

00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:33.100
but then Amy
just one day just said,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.601
"What about
Restless Leg Tour?"

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:36.534
And it didn't make
literal sense,

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:37.767
but it makes emotional sense.

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:39.901
-It does.
-And that's what we're about.

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:41.334
-It's very exciting.

00:02:41.334 --> 00:02:43.734
I mean, I've talked
at length about the fact

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.801
that now, my goodness,
I probably saw you guys

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:47.501
onstage 25 years ago in Chicago,
in a tiny, little stage.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:48.634
-Yeah.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.167
-And now you're gonna be
on these big stages.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:52.901
You have to obviously figure out
what you're going to do.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.367
You have nothing
but stories, like --

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:56.000
-Yeah, we have stories to tell

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.701
and we answer questions
and stuff.

00:02:57.701 --> 00:03:00.100
And, you know, I do think we --

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.834
You know,
Amy and I have skills that

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:03.701
we haven't shown people yet.

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.033
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:09.400
-Amy's really good at guessing
what pills people are on.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.567
[ Laughter ]
-She's really good at that.

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:11.701
-Right? You know that.

00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:14.000
-I re-- It's really --
I didn't even know

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.567
people were on pills
until I met Poehler.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.334
-Yeah. Oh, no.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:19.467
Poehler will tell you,
everyone is on pills.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:20.968
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:22.868
-Wally, you've probably
seen her do it, yeah.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:26.167
And I'm not gonna brag,
but I'm also, like,

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:27.501
pretty good at wall sits.

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:28.801
-Wall sits?

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.200
-So that might be,
like, a big part of the night,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.400
just me doing wall sits.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.734
-Wall sits being the exercise

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:34.567
where you put your back
against the wall?

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:35.767
It's, like, a quad exercise.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:36.934
-Yeah.
-Gotcha.

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:39.601
-So, I feel like
that's an hour right there.

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:41.467
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:43.534
I mean, it is very funny
to think of, like, one of those

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:45.434
old sort of, like -- What is it?

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:47.000
Those mentalist shows
where someone, like,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.300
tells a lady
what's in her purse,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.834
but it's just Amy
guessing the pills.

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:52.400
-Yes. Oh, my God. Yes.
We should do it to the audience.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.434
-And you go in the audience
and you just, like...

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:56.801
-And I touch --
I look at everyone's pills.

00:03:56.801 --> 00:03:58.567
We have a camera
that sees everyone's pills

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:00.868
before they come in.

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.567
-Yeah, you say
it's a metal detector,

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:03.701
but it's just a pill machine.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:05.734
-Sure. [ Laughs ]

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:07.667
Should we have called it
"We Know You're on Pills"?

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:10.634
[ Laughter ]

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:12.501
This is also very exciting.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:14.534
You obviously have this going
on, which is very exciting.

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:17.400
You also have --
"Mean Girls" was a movie.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.434
Then "Mean Girls"
was a musical on Broadway.

00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:22.133
And now "Mean Girls" the musical
is gonna be a movie again.

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.234
-Yes.
-Full circle.

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:27.067
-Full circle.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.767
Yep.
-And you're starting soon.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:31.200
-And we're starting March 6th.
We have an amazing cast.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.067
I'm super-excited
about this cast.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:36.100
Reneé Rapp from --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.667
Yeah, from
"Sex Lives of College Girls"

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:39.467
and is a pop star
is gonna play Regina George.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:40.801
-That's great.

00:04:40.801 --> 00:04:43.367
-Angourie Rice, who you may know
from "Mare of Easttown"

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.467
and "Senior Year" --
she's gonna play Cady.

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:48.901
Jaquel Spivey, who was on
"Strange Loop" in Broadway,

00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:50.567
Tony nominee,
is playing Damian.

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:53.367
Oh, Auli'i Cravalho --
It's just an incredible cast.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:55.200
-And, also,
don't sell yourself short.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.000
-And also [Laughs]
[Coughs] me and Tim Meadows.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.501
-Well, I do want to
give you credit.

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:06.901
Like, you and Tim had parts
in the original film

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:08.334
that you couldn't age out of,
whereas --

00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:10.033
-We couldn't age out of.
Teachers work forever.

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:11.834
I do want it to be like --
I want it to be like

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:13.968
when Gilligan from
"Gilligan's Island"

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:15.300
was at a trade show,
and you'd be like,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.067
"Oh, he looks so old
in his little hat."

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:19.367
-[ Laughs ]
That's your goal.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:20.901
-That's my goal.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.501
-What is -- And do you have to
adjust the Broadway songs

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:25.033
to the film?

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:30.100
-Yes, the songs are sounding --
It's really a more kind of pop.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.801
And it's a fascinating process,
actually, because you kind of --

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:34.234
In Broadway,
everything has to play

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:35.501
to the back of the house,
and then movies,

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.434
you can kind of come back in
and things can play

00:05:37.434 --> 00:05:38.701
really intimately.

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:42.067
And it's -- We have a very cool
directing team,

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:43.133
very cool choreographer.

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:45.868
It's gonna be...
[ Clicks tongue ]

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.033
-Wow.
-Yeah, it's gonna be good.

00:05:47.033 --> 00:05:49.133
-You do not do that many
clicks when you're lying.

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:50.801
-Unh-unh.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:52.133
-I have a ton more to ask you.

00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:54.801
We'll be right back with more
from Tina Fey, everybody!

